01. AMI

AMI = Amazon Machine Image

Think of AMI as:

A template used to create EC2 instances.

Example:

Amazon Linux 2023 AMI
        ↓
Launch EC2
        ↓
New EC2 Server

An AMI can contain:

Operating System
+
Software
+
Configuration
Example

You have configured a server:

Amazon Linux
Java 21
Docker
Nginx
Monitoring Agent
Application dependencies

You create a custom AMI.

Then:

Custom AMI
    ↓
    ├── EC2-1
    ├── EC2-2
    ├── EC2-3
    └── EC2-4

Very useful for:

Auto Scaling
Golden AMI
Disaster recovery
Standardized servers
Faster provisioning



02. Instance Types

This determines the hardware characteristics of your EC2.

Think:

Instance Type
     |
     +-- CPU
     +-- Memory
     +-- Network
     +-- Storage capabilities

Examples:

t3.micro
t3.medium
m7i.large
c7i.large
r7i.large

| Family | Purpose                     |
| ------ | --------------------------- |
| T      | Burstable/general workloads |
| M      | General purpose             |
| C      | Compute optimized           |
| R      | Memory optimized            |
| I      | Storage optimized           |
| G/P    | GPU workloads               |

Real-world example

Spring Boot application:

m7i.large

CPU-intensive workload:

c7i.large

Large in-memory application:

r7i.large

Don't memorize every instance type.

Understand why you'd choose a family.



03. vCPU and RAM

Suppose:

m7i.large

Think:

vCPU → processing power
RAM  → memory

If your application crashes because of memory exhaustion:

Application
     ↓
RAM usage increases
     ↓
OOM

You might need:

larger instance

or better application/resource management.
